Spec v1.10 — `card:teaser`: the card owns its destination

Issue #3, option A. §5.1 gains the `teaser` modifier on the card family and the
convention that makes it interoperable: when a `card:teaser`'s **last node is a
paragraph containing exactly one link**, that link is the card's destination, and
a profile makes the whole card one stretched anchor. Any other last node — prose,
a list, no link, several links — is an ordinary card: valid, unchanged, warned by
a profile that expected a destination (§7).

Why format law rather than profile territory: first-link, last-link and `href=`
answers each produce a different card from the same source, which is the
portability the format exists to prevent. Why last: the action reads at the end of
the card, and the rule mirrors the media-block rule (§4.2, "the first node is the
image") — the format grows one positional pattern instead of two. The destination
stays in the content, so a generic preview keeps the card's function; that is also
why there is no `href=` attribute.

- `demo/showcase.md`: a teaser card in "Notes and deliberate boundaries" — the
  section already contrasts an image that is not media; this is a link that is
  more than a link.
- `tools/render.js`: implements the convention — `teaserDestination()`, a single
  stretched anchor (never a second anchor wrapping the content: nested anchors are
  invalid HTML and break focus order), a renderer warning when a teaser has no
  destination, and preset lookup by *family* so `card:teaser` keeps the card class
  (`card:note`, `card:warning` were silently losing it too).
- `BRIEF.md` + both prime directives: the authoring rule, stamped v1.10.
- `Agents.md` status and `DECISIONS.md` (timeline + decision 17).

All five demos re-rendered in all three profiles (render.js changed).

Closes #3

